# Cross-Platform Dotfiles Development Rules

## Core Principles
- ALWAYS use relative paths for symlinks, never absolute paths
- ALWAYS detect OS before executing platform-specific commands
- ALWAYS test on both macOS and Linux (Ubuntu/Debian)
- NEVER hardcode platform-specific paths or commands
- ALWAYS provide fallbacks for missing tools

## Path Handling
- Use `$HOME` instead of hardcoded home directories
- Use relative paths for symlinks: `../../dotfiles/file` not `/Users/user/dotfiles/file`
- Use `realpath --relative-to` for cross-platform symlink creation
- Always expand `~` to `$HOME` in configurations

## OS Detection
- Use `uname -s` for OS detection
- Set boolean flags: `IS_MACOS=true/false`, `IS_LINUX=true/false`
- Check for WSL: `grep -q Microsoft /proc/version`
- Use `$OSTYPE` as backup detection method

## Package Managers
- macOS: Use `brew` (check `/opt/homebrew/bin/brew` first)
- Linux: Use `apt` for Ubuntu/Debian
- Always check if commands exist before installing
- Provide fallbacks for missing packages

## File Operations
- Use `sed -i ''` for macOS, `sed -i` for Linux
- Use `cp`, `mv`, `rm` with proper error handling
- Always create directories with `mkdir -p`
- Use `chmod +x` for executable files

## Shell Compatibility
- Test on both zsh and bash
- Use POSIX-compliant shell syntax when possible
- Avoid bash-specific features unless necessary
- Use `[[ ]]` for conditional tests (works in zsh and bash)

## Git Operations
- Use relative paths for git operations
- Handle different default branch names (master vs main)
- Use `git branch --unset-upstream` for upstream issues
- Always check git status before operations

## Security
- NEVER commit secrets, tokens, or API keys
- Use `.gitignore` to exclude sensitive files
- Use environment variables for secrets
- Implement secret scanning in CI/CD

## Testing Checklist
- [ ] Test on macOS (zsh)
- [ ] Test on Ubuntu/Debian (bash/zsh)
- [ ] Test on WSL2
- [ ] Verify symlinks work correctly
- [ ] Check package installation works
- [ ] Verify git operations work
- [ ] Test error handling

## Common Issues to Avoid
- Hardcoded paths like `/Users/username/`
- Platform-specific commands without detection
- Missing error handling for failed operations
- Symlinks pointing to wrong locations
- Package manager conflicts

## Best Practices
- Use functions for reusable code
- Implement proper logging with colors
- Provide clear error messages
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Document platform-specific behavior 